INT. LOCKER ROOM - DAY

A cinderblock locker room under a low ceiling. Benches bolted to the floor. A whiteboard with a play half erased. The air smells of liniment and wet tape. They lost by one point, and nobody has moved to the showers.

COACH ELLIOT PRUITT, 50s, sits on the bench by the door with his cap in his hands. At the far locker, still in uniform, sits RAFAEL "RAFI" DELGADO, 24, the point guard, staring into his open locker. His backup, DUKE ANDERSON, 22, towers over the bench with a towel around his neck. And the team captain, MARCUS WYNN, 28, leans against the whiteboard with his arms crossed over a ruined jersey.

COACH PRUITT
(to all of them)
Listen up.

Nobody does. Rafi pulls his shoe off, studies it, puts it back on.

COACH PRUITT (CONT'D)
(louder)
I said listen up.

DUKE
We're listening.

MARCUS
We're listening, Coach.

RAFI
(muttering to himself)
Should've passed.

COACH PRUITT
What was that, Rafi?

RAFI
Nothing.

MARCUS
He said he should've passed.

RAFI
I said nothing.

DUKE
You had Marcus open.

RAFI
Marcus was not open.

MARCUS
I was wide open.

RAFI
You were covered.

MARCUS
By a guy half my size.

COACH PRUITT
Enough.

DUKE
(overlapping)
He had the lane.

RAFI
(overlapping)
It was a double team.

MARCUS
(overlapping)
It was one guy.

COACH PRUITT
I said enough!

His voice cracks against the cinderblock. The three of them go still. Coach Pruitt puts his cap on, takes it off, sets it on his knee.

COACH PRUITT (CONT'D)
(quietly)
That was my call. The last play. I drew it up. Not Rafi's.

Rafi looks at him. Marcus uncrosses his arms.

MARCUS
Coach.

COACH PRUITT
Nobody's showering yet. We sit here for five minutes and nobody says one word.

DUKE
Five minutes?

COACH PRUITT
Duke, that's a word.

Duke sits. Rafi takes off the other shoe. Marcus slides down the whiteboard until he is on the floor. Coach Pruitt looks at the clock.

COACH PRUITT (CONT'D)
(to himself)
Four fifty-eight.
